  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of a magnetar surrounded by an infalling gaseous disk, showing how two different kinds of precession can change the system’s orientation. At the center is the magnetar, drawn with strong dipolar magnetic field lines and a vertical spin axis. Around it sits a glowing, tilted disk of gas that appears to rotate and emit radiation.\n\nThe main idea is that the disk and the magnetar’s magnetic field are not perfectly aligned with the spin axis, so they can wobble or precess. One indicated motion is Lense–Thirring precession of the gaseous disk, shown as the disk’s axis tracing a circular path around the spin axis. Another is magnetar magnetic-field precession, in which the magnetic axis itself rotates around the spin axis. The lower rendering appears to show a different alignment or phase of these motions, emphasizing that the observable geometry can change over time.\n\nOverall, the image illustrates a compact star–disk system in which relativistic frame dragging and magnetic misalignment both influence the orientation of the disk, field, and likely the direction of emitted radiation.",
